  annotation "note_1771589091418_0", anchor: "third turning since the last orca sighting", text: "Frozen Peace chronology measured in predator absence. The filing clerks date their paperwork by the gap between attacks, which tells you everything about what 'peace' means on the Berg. Three turnings is long enough for a committee to commission a treatise. It is not long enough for anyone to stop counting.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771589091418_1", anchor: "two cups set out; one untouched", text: "The Office classifies this as atmospheric detail. The Office is wrong. Penguins who practice deep-source arts — particularly those involving sustained perception-alteration — report presences. Not hallucinations. Presences. Patient, attentive, at the edge of sight. The Nightshelf call them the watchers and claim they predate penguin habitation. The Aurora call them symptoms. I have catalogued impressions in the Foundation Depths left by appendages that are not flippers, arranged in patterns I cannot explain and have stopped trying to. ☽'Cilocum set out two cups because the Chancellor was not alone in that room. Whether what sat with her was a degradation artifact or something that had been waiting in the deep ice for an invitation to be perceived — that is the question this footnote accidentally asks and the Office deliberately declines to answer.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589091418_2", anchor: "Jark Rankzule Gnarkzund Zaith Rend Kzulrora and Krathund Gnurl all deceased", text: "Four names. All orca. The treatise uses their deaths to demonstrate that Mandatum∴absconditu fails at containment, but walks past the staggering implication without blinking: orcas stole a Nightshelf grimoire four times across two eras. These were not food raids. They did not eat the Lantern. They wanted to *read* it. Every popular account of the Orca Incursion I have annotated treats orca motivation as predatory — shore-breakers, herders, finishers, the brutal arithmetic of hunt-songs. Here are four orcas who died for a book. The modern scholar's framework has no room for orca scholarship. How extraordinary that the evidence keeps insisting.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771589091418_3", anchor: "Including, necessarily, the author's", text: "Footnote seven. Seven words. The entire epistemological foundation of the treatise, dismantled by its own author in a subordinate clause. I have never seen a document so calmly confess that it might not be what it thinks it is.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
